\title{A Formally Verified Structural Core for Integrated Information ($\Phi$)}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 24, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Integrated Information Theory (IIT) proposes a scalar invariant $\Phi$ (``phi'')
that quantifies the \emph{irreducibility} of a system of interacting elements:
the degree to which the whole generates information above and beyond the
information generated independently by its parts. We isolate and rigorously
establish the combinatorial--order-theoretic skeleton on which every concrete
formulation of $\Phi$ rests. Working over a finite element set
$\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, we define the lattice of nontrivial bipartitions
(``cuts''), model a system as an arbitrary nonnegative
\emph{effective-information functional} $\ei$ on cuts, and define the integrated
information $\Phi$ as the value of $\ei$ at the \emph{Minimum Information
Partition} (MIP), i.e.\ the minimum of $\ei$ over all cuts. We prove: (i) the
MIP exists and realizes $\Phi$; (ii) $\Phi$ is the greatest lower bound of the
effective-information landscape; (iii) $\Phi \ge 0$; (iv) the reducibility
characterization $\Phi = 0 \iff$ some cut destroys no information;
(v) monotonicity of $\Phi$ in the functional $\ei$; and (vi) a shared-bottleneck
rigidity result equating $\Phi$ across systems that agree at a common minimizing
cut. Every theorem depends only on the nonnegativity of $\ei$, so the entire
structure transfers verbatim to any concrete information measure. All results
have been formalized and machine-checked.
\end{abstract}

